  "answer": "## Root cause: a module-level “hydration” flag shared across all `useMediaQuery` instances\n\nIn `packages/material-ui/src/useMediaQuery/useMediaQuery.js`, Material-UI uses a **module-scoped** variable:\n\n```js\nlet hydrationCompleted = false;\n```\n\nBecause it’s not per-component (or per React tree), it’s effectively **global state** for the whole document/runtime. The exported helper `testReset()` also mutates that same flag:\n\n```js\nexport function testReset() {\n  hydrationCompleted = false;\n}\n```  \n(`packages/material-ui/src/useMediaQuery/useMediaQuery.js`)\n\n## How it flips too early in mixed render/hydrate flows\n\n### Initial `useState` can read the flag\nDuring render, the hook initializes `match` like this:\n\n- If `(hydrationCompleted || noSsr) && supportMatchMedia` it immediately uses `window.matchMedia(query).matches`\n- Otherwise it falls back to `ssrMatchMedia` or `defaultMatches`\n\n(`packages/material-ui/src/useMediaQuery/useMediaQuery.js`)\n\n### `useEffect` sets the shared flag on mount\nAfter the hook’s component mounts, it runs an effect that does:\n\n```js\nReact.useEffect(() => {\n  let active = true;\n  hydrationCompleted = true;\n  ...\n}, [query, supportMatchMedia]);\n```\n\nSo **the first mounted instance** of `useMediaQuery` will set `hydrationCompleted = true`, even if other parts of the app have not been hydrated yet. (`packages/material-ui/src/useMediaQuery/useMediaQuery.js`)\n\n## Why this produces SSR/client mismatches\n\nIf app “1” renders and mounts `useMediaQuery` early, it will set the global flag to `true`. Then app “2”, which is still waiting to hydrate, will render `useMediaQuery` with `hydrationCompleted === true`, causing it to compute `match` from `window.matchMedia` immediately—potentially producing different classes/props and triggering `className` hydration mismatches.\n\nThis explains why load-order changes or manually resetting the flag can “fix” the behavior: they control when that shared `hydrationCompleted` flips.",
